README-KEEP -- taking things off this collection================================================    keep <name>...      the program, and whatever it needs    keep -n <name>...   dry run: say what would be written, touch nothing    keep -f <name>...   overwrite files already there (otherwise refused)    keep -s <name>...   take the SOURCE tree as well, where there is one    keep -p <dir> ...   put it all under <dir> instead of /dd    kept                every file keep has written, and the total    unkeep <name>...    remove what keep wrote for <name>    unkeep -n <name>... dry runFirst: you may not need this at all-----------------------------------If you mount this collection as /dd -- the recommended arrangement, and whatDOC/README-RUNNING is about -- then nothing needs keeping.  Every program isalready where it expects to be, and 258 of them read data from /dd that isright here.  Run them in place and be done.`keep' is for the other case: you ALREADY RUN OS-9, /dd is YOUR system, andthis collection is mounted alongside as another drive.  Then a program thatreads /dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz will look on your disk and not find it.  `keep' iswhat puts it there -- the program, and the files it reads, in the places itlooks for them.That is the whole job: make a program that expects the collection at /dd workon a /dd that is not the collection.Where it copies from and to---------------------------It finds the collection by looking for DOC/DEPENDS on /h0, /h1, /h2 and /dd,in that order, and destinations default to /dd.  To say otherwise:    -p <dir>            on the command line    KEEP_FROM, KEEP_TO  in the environmentSet those INSIDE an OS-9 session -- `export KEEP_TO=/h1' at a shell here.A variable set in the shell that launched os9exec does not reach an OS-9program; that is a property of the emulator, not of these commands.Taking the source too---------------------`keep -s' brings the program's source tree along with it.  Where the sourcesurvived, you get it; where it did not, keep says so and takes the rest.Much of this collection has no source at all -- that is not a fault, it iswhat reached us.The tree is named by ARCHIVE, not by program.  SRC/divutils holds `gen',`run' and `if'; SRC/adv holds `advent'.  Guessing SRC/<program> findsnothing for most of the disk, so keep looks the name up in DOC/ORIGINS.`about <program>' shows you the same answer before you commit to anything.What `keep' takes with it--------------------------Not just the binary.  DOC/DEPENDS lists, for every program, each absolutepath its code contains -- generated by scanning the binaries, not byguesswork -- and keep brings any of those that exist here.    keep cookie     takes cookie, its sayings and its sayhash    keep fortune    takes fortune and its fortunes.dat    keep hack       takes hack, and tells you about the playgroundA dependency that is a DIRECTORY is skipped and said so out loud: hack's/h0/games/hack/playground is a directory of bones files and scores, andcopying it is your decision, not ours.A file named twice is taken once.  A program that wants both /dd/sys/termcapand /h0/sys/termcap is asking for one file.Termcap is not copied at all, and does not need to be: SYS/login exportsTERMCAP, and every program here that wants termcap reads that variable first.That was measured, program by program.Nothing is overwritten.  If the destination already has a file of that name,keep refuses, names it, and moves on -- your own `ls' is never replaced byours.  `-f' overrides that.Data files are written PUBLICLY WRITABLE, and that matters.  Everything hereis owned 0.0, so anyone logged in as somebody else is never the owner andonly the public write bit counts.  A read-only score file is a game thatcannot save, and testing as 0.0 hides it, because RBF gives the super-user abypass.Why `unkeep' is safe--------------------Every file keep writes is recorded in /dd/SYS/kept: the program it came for,where it went, its size, and its checksum.unkeep reads that receipt and, for each file:    gone already        says so, moves on    checksum matches    removes it    CHECKSUM CHANGED    KEEPS IT, and tells youSo a file you have written to is, by definition, no longer the file keep putthere -- and unkeep will not touch it.  Your saves, your scores, your editedconfiguration survive `unkeep', by construction rather than by our beingcareful.  Demonstrated:    $ keep fortune    collection at /h0, keeping into /dd    fortune:      /dd/CMDS/fortune      /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat    2 files written.    $ echo "A fortune I added myself." >> /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat    $ unkeep fortune    fortune:      removed /dd/CMDS/fortune      !! /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat has CHANGED since it was kept -- left alone         (this is how your saves and high scores survive an unkeep)    1 removed, 1 left alone.A file unkeep declines to remove stays on the receipt, so `kept' still knowsabout it and a later `unkeep' will offer it again.The checksum is the OS-9 module CRC -- polynomial $800063, complemented --used here on ordinary files too.  Any checksum would do; that one is alreadythe collection's idiom and its other tools understand it.How they are written--------------------Three OS-9 modules, built from one source, SRC/keep/keep.c, with `cc -qm' sothey carry no trap handler and need no cio.  They fork directly, like anyother command here.They began as bash scripts, and those are kept in SRC/keep as keep.sh,unkeep.sh and kept.sh.  They were replaced because a script could not do theone job that matters: it needs bash and /dd/tmp ON THE DESTINATION disk, andthe destination is somebody else's OS-9 system that has neither.  Run againsta /dd that was not the collection, the script version copied nothing and saidalmost nothing about why.  This bash is also a difficult thing to writeagainst -- command substitution does not inherit PATH or see shell functions,the -f and -d tests are unreliable on a host-directory mount andcase-sensitive where OS-9 is not, there is no /dev/null, and mkdir has no -p.The C version has none of those problems.